1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,360

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:09,280
All right, so hello, guys,
and welcome back to CS25.

3
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:13,280
Today, I'm a very glad
to welcome Victoria

4
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:16,720
Lin from Thinking Machines Lab.

5
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,960
So she's a member
of technical staff

6
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:23,200
there, where she focuses on
native multimodal intelligence.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,320
And she is passionate about
building AI systems that

8
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:30,640
empower humans to tackle
complex, knowledge-intensive

9
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:32,479
problems effectively.

10
00:00:32,479 --> 00:00:34,600
And previously, she was
a research scientist

11
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:38,200
at Meta AI and
Salesforce AI Research.

12
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:42,460
And she received her PhD from
the University of Washington.

13
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:45,760
And without further ado,
let's give it up for Victoria.

14
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:46,320
[APPLAUSE]

15
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:49,440
Thank you for having me.

16
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:52,600
So can you hear me OK?

17
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,880
Yeah, so I'm very
glad to be here

18
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:59,220
today and talk about native
multimodal language models.

19
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:04,660
So a declaration is this talk is
going to be exclusively focused

20
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:07,040
on public materials.

21
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:12,900
And the talk represents my own
opinion, not of my employer.

22
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:16,500
So without further
ado, let's start it.

23
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:20,080
So I think without
much argument,

24
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:23,900
large language models is one of
the most exciting breakthrough

25
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:26,740
happened in the past few years.

26
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:30,980
I think nowadays, probably
everyone sitting in this room

27
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:35,220
will use language
models a little bit,

28
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:37,460
like during your daily life.

29
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,420
So for example, like asking
questions through ChatGPT,

30
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:44,700
using Cloud Code, or Codex to
help you to code your project.

31
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:48,500
So these models were built
based on next token prediction

32
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:50,520
over symbolic information.

33
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:57,970
And they scale to demonstrate
amazing emergent capabilities.

34
00:01:57,970 --> 00:02:01,010
So just as a quick recap.

35
00:02:01,010 --> 00:02:05,410
How does today's large
language model paradigms works?

36
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:07,070
So here's architecture.

37
00:02:07,070 --> 00:02:08,509
You will be familiar with it.

38
00:02:08,509 --> 00:02:10,210
This is a transformer cell.

39
00:02:10,210 --> 00:02:13,890
So basically, it carries
out next token prediction.

40
00:02:13,890 --> 00:02:19,570
Predict the probability
distributed over a vocabulary.

41
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:26,090
And we train such a model
using a large number of corpus

42
00:02:26,090 --> 00:02:29,010
curated over different domains.

43
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:33,330
And as we noticed that as
the data and model scales,

44
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:36,490
they start to demonstrate
amazing capabilities such

45
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:39,130
as they can acquire a
large amount of knowledge.

46
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:41,930
They can be post-trained
to follow instructions.

47
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:44,950
And they are able to carry out
a train of thought reasoning.

48
00:02:44,950 --> 00:02:48,290
And most recently, we also
found they are actually

49
00:02:48,290 --> 00:02:52,810
able to plan to solve
complicated tasks.

50
00:02:52,810 --> 00:02:56,890
However, I'm also going to argue
that just the language modeling

51
00:02:56,890 --> 00:02:59,550
alone is not enough for us.

52
00:02:59,550 --> 00:03:00,990
So why is it?

53
00:03:00,990 --> 00:03:05,210
It is because both the digital
world we deal with every day

54
00:03:05,210 --> 00:03:07,610
and also the physical
world we live in,

55
00:03:07,610 --> 00:03:09,870
they are actually filled
with information coming

56
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:12,030
from different modalities.

57
00:03:12,030 --> 00:03:15,510
Like images, audio, video.

58
00:03:15,510 --> 00:03:18,630
So if you surf on
the internet, you

59
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:20,230
will get what I'm talking about.

60
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:23,590
And also, suppose if we're
building AI systems that's

61
00:03:23,590 --> 00:03:27,450
going to interact with us real
time in the physical world,

62
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:33,230
we have to deal with
multimodal information.

63
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:35,270
So the goal is to
build basically

64
00:03:35,270 --> 00:03:38,330
AI systems that's not only
processing symbolic knowledge,

65
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:41,990
but it's also able
to seamlessly handle

66
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:44,970
these multimodal
information sharing to us,

67
00:03:44,970 --> 00:03:49,150
in terms of visual and
auditory information.

68
00:03:49,150 --> 00:03:51,150
And actually, if
you pay attention

69
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:54,870
to today's state of the art
language model landscape,

70
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:58,910
you will see, for a lot of
recent models being rolled out,

71
00:03:58,910 --> 00:04:02,510
they have already been claiming
that these models are native,

72
00:04:02,510 --> 00:04:07,290
multimodal, and
are able to process

73
00:04:07,290 --> 00:04:10,850
large amount of multimodal
information, which is true.

74
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:14,090
We see very amazing
capabilities from these models

75
00:04:14,090 --> 00:04:17,010
in terms of image,
video understanding,

76
00:04:17,010 --> 00:04:19,930
visual genetic
coding, and so on.

77
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:22,770
So the first part
of this talk is just

78
00:04:22,770 --> 00:04:28,890
going to dive into the building
philosophies of such models.

79
00:04:28,890 --> 00:04:34,450
So what do we define as native
multimodal language models?

80
00:04:34,450 --> 00:04:36,730
I myself personally
find it easier

81
00:04:36,730 --> 00:04:39,930
to understand this
paradigm by thinking

82
00:04:39,930 --> 00:04:42,930
with an analogy of
language models.

83
00:04:42,930 --> 00:04:46,650
So the state of the art
multimodal language model

84
00:04:46,650 --> 00:04:51,410
architecture is really
performing tokenization

85
00:04:51,410 --> 00:04:55,960
across the board, which means
whether the incoming information

86
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:58,500
comes as image modality,
video modality,

87
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:01,080
or is a piece of audio,
we try to find a way

88
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:05,280
to convert these signals into
token information that can be

89
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:07,800
processed by the transformer.

90
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:12,640
And on top of that, we perform
global autoregressive generation

91
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:16,400
modeling similar
to language models.

92
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:18,360
So exactly what do I mean?

93
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:20,880
So for text, we
can tokenize them

94
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:23,820
similar as what we do for
large language models.

95
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:26,600
Usually, we can use
byte pair encoding.

96
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:30,480
Break down the text into
small pieces of tokens.

97
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:34,000
And for images, turns out we
can also do similar things.

98
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,320
So when a whole piece
of image coming in,

99
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:40,500
we can perform an operation
which we call the patchify.

100
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:44,200
So basically, we can divide
this image into small parts.

101
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:50,040
And parts is usually predefined
by a standard size, such as 16

102
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:52,080
pixels times 16 pixels.

103
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:58,320
So once we standardize the image
units, we can run, for example,

104
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:00,700
encoders on these
image units and get

105
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:02,500
vector representations of them.

106
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:05,300
And then we sequentialize
these vectorization

107
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:09,300
of the image units, and then
we got the image tokens.

108
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:12,780
For audio, we can perform
similar operation.

109
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:16,020
When the waveform comes in,
we can perform transforms

110
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:17,540
on top of the waveform.

111
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:21,500
And we can apply certain
operations basically

112
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:24,180
to tokenize the
resulting representation.

113
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:28,600
And accordingly, you can encode
your audio tokens as well.

114
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,180

115
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:32,600
And for videos.

116
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:34,200
So this slide is dense.

117
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:36,960
But what really is
saying is for videos,

118
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:40,980
we can also easily seen them
as a sequence of images.

119
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:45,320
So if we think of a video
segment as a sequence of image,

120
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:48,220
we can perform a
similar petrification

121
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:49,980
on each of the video frame.

122
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:53,850
And then we
concatenate the patches

123
00:06:53,850 --> 00:06:56,230
of all the images in
this video sequence,

124
00:06:56,230 --> 00:06:58,490
and then we can still
represent the video

125
00:06:58,490 --> 00:07:00,290
as a sequence of tokens.

126
00:07:00,290 --> 00:07:04,890
So when I say tokens, they are
not necessarily discrete tokens.

127
00:07:04,890 --> 00:07:06,570
If we encode them
into a dense vector,

128
00:07:06,570 --> 00:07:10,290
we still refer to
them as tokens.

129
00:07:10,290 --> 00:07:13,350
And basically, we perform this
tokenization across a board.

130
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:16,610
And then we can basically
getting multimodal information

131
00:07:16,610 --> 00:07:17,530
sequence.

132
00:07:17,530 --> 00:07:19,130
We can train them
in the same way

133
00:07:19,130 --> 00:07:22,250
as we train a large
language model.

134
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:25,570
So for state of the art
multimodal language models,

135
00:07:25,570 --> 00:07:27,570
when we speak of
them, usually there

136
00:07:27,570 --> 00:07:29,770
are two broad types of them.

137
00:07:29,770 --> 00:07:33,810
So one type of multimodal
models consider multimodality

138
00:07:33,810 --> 00:07:37,330
as input, but only text
modality as output.

139
00:07:37,330 --> 00:07:41,050
So as is shown here, so we have
a multimodal sequence coming in.

140
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:43,610
We condition them, but we
only calculate the losses

141
00:07:43,610 --> 00:07:45,130
on the text tokens.

142
00:07:45,130 --> 00:07:49,730
So this can induce amazing
understanding abilities.

143
00:07:49,730 --> 00:07:52,490
Like we have an image as
input, video as input.

144
00:07:52,490 --> 00:07:55,010
The model can start to
answer questions in text.

145
00:07:55,010 --> 00:07:58,950

146
00:07:58,950 --> 00:08:02,450
And in fact, lots of
multimodal language models,

147
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:04,310
you are probably
familiarized today

148
00:08:04,310 --> 00:08:09,210
like Gemini, Qwen, and Kimi,
they operate in this mode.

149
00:08:09,210 --> 00:08:12,930
So which means it's multimodal
input and text only output.

150
00:08:12,930 --> 00:08:15,190
Of course, I think these
companies have also

151
00:08:15,190 --> 00:08:18,470
developed other models
which is able to generate

152
00:08:18,470 --> 00:08:19,930
multimodal information.

153
00:08:19,930 --> 00:08:28,030
But a lot of the core products
is doing text only output.

154
00:08:28,030 --> 00:08:31,270
So there is another
categories of models, which

155
00:08:31,270 --> 00:08:33,470
is called the Omni models.

156
00:08:33,470 --> 00:08:37,270
So which means they not only
take multimodal information

157
00:08:37,270 --> 00:08:40,150
as input, but they also
do multimodal information

158
00:08:40,150 --> 00:08:41,309
as output.

159
00:08:41,309 --> 00:08:44,310
In this case, these models,
is capable of generating

160
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:49,110
not only text, but
also images and audios.

161
00:08:49,110 --> 00:08:53,290
So something like you might be
familiar with GPT-4 0, which

162
00:08:53,290 --> 00:08:56,190
is named Omni, because it
can also generate images.

163
00:08:56,190 --> 00:08:59,810

164
00:08:59,810 --> 00:09:04,130
So this is an important
slide for today,

165
00:09:04,130 --> 00:09:08,890
because I think this summarized
that by taking this tokenization

166
00:09:08,890 --> 00:09:12,650
view, how we could transfer
a lot of the architecture

167
00:09:12,650 --> 00:09:15,890
and training principles from the
large language modeling paradigm

168
00:09:15,890 --> 00:09:17,770
to the multimodal paradigm.

169
00:09:17,770 --> 00:09:20,850
So there are a couple of
benefits we can get from it.

170
00:09:20,850 --> 00:09:25,510
So first, by designing
such architectures,

171
00:09:25,510 --> 00:09:28,930
this allows us to build
multimodal models, which

172
00:09:28,930 --> 00:09:33,090
is able to allow us to conduct
prompting and instruction

173
00:09:33,090 --> 00:09:34,170
following.

174
00:09:34,170 --> 00:09:37,530
So we can prompt these models
with mixed model prompts

175
00:09:37,530 --> 00:09:41,970
and and enable them to solve
complicated tasks which

176
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:45,930
requires multimodality.

177
00:09:45,930 --> 00:09:49,240
This model will also acquire
planning and reasoning

178
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:50,060
capabilities.

179
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:52,080
And now, even more
amazingly, they

180
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:54,500
can plan and reason with
multimodal information.

181
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:57,640

182
00:09:57,640 --> 00:10:00,640
So another exciting
thing is we also

183
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:03,040
observed that some positive
learnings from large language

184
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:06,320
modeling, including scaling
data and model size, that

185
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:08,660
can lead to performance
improvement,

186
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:10,940
can also be transferred
to multimodal.

187
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:15,200
Which means for these models,
we see significant promise

188
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:18,920
that if we further scale up the
number of parameters in them

189
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:21,113
and also we scale up
the training database,

190
00:10:21,113 --> 00:10:23,280
their performance is going
to get better and better,

191
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:26,080
and we're going to see
more emergent capabilities.

192
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:28,840
So the only interesting
remaining here

193
00:10:28,840 --> 00:10:33,760
is I think unlike text modeling,
which has very accurate scaling

194
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:34,260
laws.

195
00:10:34,260 --> 00:10:38,120
So basically people is able to
derive the power law equation

196
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:42,660
and fit the coefficients
of the equation,

197
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:46,000
I think the exact scaling
law study for multimodal

198
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,300
is underexplored.

199
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:51,220
But on a rough trend,
we are still seeing

200
00:10:51,220 --> 00:10:53,060
this scaling capability.

201
00:10:53,060 --> 00:10:56,620
So I think, for
example, trying to find

202
00:10:56,620 --> 00:10:58,380
the exact fit of
multimodal scaling

203
00:10:58,380 --> 00:11:02,540
law could be a very interesting
future research topic.

204
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:07,060
The force is a lot of
architectural scaling techniques

205
00:11:07,060 --> 00:11:11,300
and also efficient
techniques, such as mixture

206
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:14,460
of experts and
these variations, we

207
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:18,700
can also apply them directly
to multimodal language models

208
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:21,640
to achieve better
scaling performance.

209
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:25,340

210
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:30,060
So next, I basically
want to talk

211
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:34,620
about the second category
of multimodal models, which

212
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:37,580
are the Omni models.

213
00:11:37,580 --> 00:11:41,500
Because my past
research group has

214
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:43,480
been dedicated to
developing such models,

215
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:47,080
and I want to present a
few variations of these.

216
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:51,800
So when we come to Omni models,
because they can generate

217
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:54,200
not only text modality.

218
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:57,440
So there are a few key
research questions.

219
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:00,720
So the first one is, how do
we enable them to generate

220
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:04,040
the non-text modalities?

221
00:12:04,040 --> 00:12:08,400
And the second is, is
there a possible transfer

222
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:10,480
between understanding
and generation

223
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:12,180
of the non-text modalities?

224
00:12:12,180 --> 00:12:15,640

225
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:18,680
So with these questions
in mind, the first model

226
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:21,960
I'm going to talk about
is the Chameleon family.

227
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:27,420
So these models adopt a
very simple hypothesis.

228
00:12:27,420 --> 00:12:29,760
So basically, the
question to ask

229
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:33,280
is, can we actually
simply tokenize

230
00:12:33,280 --> 00:12:37,480
every modality, converting
them into discrete tokens?

231
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:39,620
This is a very powerful view.

232
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:41,400
If it is successful,
basically, it

233
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:44,990
will allow us to model
the multimodal world

234
00:12:44,990 --> 00:12:47,010
as a sequence of
discrete tokens,

235
00:12:47,010 --> 00:12:49,670
so that lots of properties
of language models

236
00:12:49,670 --> 00:12:51,870
could potentially transfer.

237
00:12:51,870 --> 00:12:54,030
So this is basically
the hypothesis

238
00:12:54,030 --> 00:12:55,830
with the Chameleon models.

239
00:12:55,830 --> 00:13:00,550
And the key here is, how
do we discretize images?

240
00:13:00,550 --> 00:13:03,370
And it turns out, it's
not that difficult.

241
00:13:03,370 --> 00:13:06,790
So remember the patchified
view we just talked about.

242
00:13:06,790 --> 00:13:09,470
So for Chameleon,
basically we just

243
00:13:09,470 --> 00:13:13,590
apply another step of
vectorization of these image

244
00:13:13,590 --> 00:13:14,330
patches.

245
00:13:14,330 --> 00:13:17,950
So how do we do that
is given an image,

246
00:13:17,950 --> 00:13:20,750
we convert them into patches.

247
00:13:20,750 --> 00:13:23,790
And then basically, we
run a continuous encoders

248
00:13:23,790 --> 00:13:27,110
on top of them to get the
embeddings of these patches.

249
00:13:27,110 --> 00:13:29,810
And once we get these
patches, we don't stop there.

250
00:13:29,810 --> 00:13:33,250

251
00:13:33,250 --> 00:13:36,950
We follow it up with a
learned vector codebook.

252
00:13:36,950 --> 00:13:40,030
Then basically, we can take
our computed embeddings

253
00:13:40,030 --> 00:13:42,410
and try to match
them with a codebook,

254
00:13:42,410 --> 00:13:48,170
basically to get to the closest
discrete correspondence of each

255
00:13:48,170 --> 00:13:49,570
of the patches.

256
00:13:49,570 --> 00:13:52,650
And by this way, basically,
we can convert an image

257
00:13:52,650 --> 00:13:56,610
into a sequence of
discrete representation.

258
00:13:56,610 --> 00:13:59,010
And that's how we
tokenize the image, which

259
00:13:59,010 --> 00:14:04,770
is really by finding
their corresponding index

260
00:14:04,770 --> 00:14:07,290
in the codebook.

261
00:14:07,290 --> 00:14:10,450
So basically, we
discretize the images

262
00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:13,730
using the VQ-VAE technique.

263
00:14:13,730 --> 00:14:16,490
And then we put the
image and text together

264
00:14:16,490 --> 00:14:18,650
as an interleaved sequence.

265
00:14:18,650 --> 00:14:21,770
And then basically, we apply
cross-entropy language model

266
00:14:21,770 --> 00:14:23,890
training objective on this.

267
00:14:23,890 --> 00:14:26,130
So the result of
Chameleon model, basically

268
00:14:26,130 --> 00:14:31,210
found that it is able to
generate interleaved text

269
00:14:31,210 --> 00:14:33,790
and image in arbitrary order.

270
00:14:33,790 --> 00:14:38,610
So here, I'm showing a sequence
of image and text generated

271
00:14:38,610 --> 00:14:39,702
by this model.

272
00:14:39,702 --> 00:14:41,410
So basically, you can
see we can actually

273
00:14:41,410 --> 00:14:46,590
generate mixed model documents
from this model directly.

274
00:14:46,590 --> 00:14:49,390
And with this
representation, it will also

275
00:14:49,390 --> 00:14:52,950
allow us to perform
multitasking similar as how

276
00:14:52,950 --> 00:14:54,850
we do with our language models.

277
00:14:54,850 --> 00:14:57,990
So basically, we can give these
models mixed model prompts

278
00:14:57,990 --> 00:15:02,950
and get output from it,
such as we can chat with it,

279
00:15:02,950 --> 00:15:08,670
do brainstorming, compare
images, and so on.

280
00:15:08,670 --> 00:15:11,630
So this is a model
that purely trying

281
00:15:11,630 --> 00:15:15,710
to apply the large language
model technique to mixed model

282
00:15:15,710 --> 00:15:17,350
modeling.

283
00:15:17,350 --> 00:15:19,990
So it is one of the
first model to show

284
00:15:19,990 --> 00:15:23,830
that if we train from scratch
interleaved text and image

285
00:15:23,830 --> 00:15:27,630
sequences, it can induce
multimodal capabilities

286
00:15:27,630 --> 00:15:32,390
while preserving the strong
text only performance.

287
00:15:32,390 --> 00:15:35,430
However, we also
realize some limitations

288
00:15:35,430 --> 00:15:37,610
when we discretize the image.

289
00:15:37,610 --> 00:15:41,280
So basically, one is for
image understanding tasks,

290
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:45,340
discretization often cause
significant information loss.

291
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:47,860
So as a result,
when we compare it

292
00:15:47,860 --> 00:15:49,900
to the state of the
art multimodal language

293
00:15:49,900 --> 00:15:53,420
models nowadays, which is
running a continuous image

294
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:56,220
encoding such as
SigLIP, there is usually

295
00:15:56,220 --> 00:16:00,420
a significant performance gap
in terms of image understanding.

296
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:03,300
And the second is even
on the generative side,

297
00:16:03,300 --> 00:16:10,420
we also found that basically
discretizing the images

298
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:14,020
and generate them actually
poses some challenges in terms

299
00:16:14,020 --> 00:16:15,560
of token efficiency.

300
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:18,660
So these models, which
means these models typically

301
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:20,940
needs to be trained on
large amounts of data

302
00:16:20,940 --> 00:16:24,180
to be able to sample
well-formed images.

303
00:16:24,180 --> 00:16:28,980
So in summary, Chameleon
presented a very interesting

304
00:16:28,980 --> 00:16:33,740
paradigm where we can train
text and images interleaved

305
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:35,300
from scratch.

306
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:38,060
But we also found
the limitations.

307
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:42,040
Basically the assumption that
we can discretize information

308
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:43,660
in the images might
be too strong.

309
00:16:43,660 --> 00:16:46,560

310
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:51,420
So transfusion is
another approach,

311
00:16:51,420 --> 00:16:55,760
which is proposed in roughly
the similar time as Chameleon.

312
00:16:55,760 --> 00:16:58,520
So which basically tries
to actually overcome

313
00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:00,520
some of the limitations
of Chameleon.

314
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:03,600
So transfusion is a very
interesting and also impactful

315
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:05,359
architecture.

316
00:17:05,359 --> 00:17:12,119
So basically, it tries to
adopt continuous representation

317
00:17:12,119 --> 00:17:14,980
of the images,
using them as input,

318
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:17,240
but also tries to
directly predict

319
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:20,000
these continuous
representation of images.

320
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:25,000
So how do we predict continuous
representation of images?

321
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:29,160
So if you are familiar with
diffusion models, so basically,

322
00:17:29,160 --> 00:17:33,160
diffusion models is a
very successful approach

323
00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:34,800
for image generation.

324
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:40,390
And it conducts image generation
by starting from a noise,

325
00:17:40,390 --> 00:17:43,310
and continuously
predict the noise

326
00:17:43,310 --> 00:17:45,750
to be removed from
this initial noise

327
00:17:45,750 --> 00:17:49,670
until you arrived
at a clear picture.

328
00:17:49,670 --> 00:17:51,230
So basically what
transcription does

329
00:17:51,230 --> 00:17:54,830
is it combines autoregressive
language modeling

330
00:17:54,830 --> 00:17:57,230
and diffusion based
image generation

331
00:17:57,230 --> 00:17:59,590
in a single transformer.

332
00:17:59,590 --> 00:18:01,990
So the input to a
transfusion model

333
00:18:01,990 --> 00:18:05,510
is still an interleaved
text and image sequence.

334
00:18:05,510 --> 00:18:09,110
And for the text, we perform
standard autoregressive

335
00:18:09,110 --> 00:18:10,390
modeling.

336
00:18:10,390 --> 00:18:18,070
But for the image part, instead
of autoregressive prediction,

337
00:18:18,070 --> 00:18:21,510
we take a segment of
image representation

338
00:18:21,510 --> 00:18:25,630
and basically try to do
diffusion operation on it.

339
00:18:25,630 --> 00:18:29,990
The model can perform
multiple step of diffusion.

340
00:18:29,990 --> 00:18:34,047
Get the clear image and then
take the clear image as input

341
00:18:34,047 --> 00:18:35,130
and move autoregressively.

342
00:18:35,130 --> 00:18:41,950
So some important architectural
changes related is for text,

343
00:18:41,950 --> 00:18:45,330
we can continue to use this
causal attention structure.

344
00:18:45,330 --> 00:18:50,970
And for the images, for the
sake of better performance,

345
00:18:50,970 --> 00:18:55,590
we apply bidirectional attention
on top of these images.

346
00:18:55,590 --> 00:19:00,450
So this is the
transformation architecture.

347
00:19:00,450 --> 00:19:06,770
So basically as shown in
the transfusion paper,

348
00:19:06,770 --> 00:19:10,050
so these models is able
to quickly generate

349
00:19:10,050 --> 00:19:12,290
much better image
quality compared

350
00:19:12,290 --> 00:19:18,130
to discrete token-based
image generation using

351
00:19:18,130 --> 00:19:22,130
much less token budget.

352
00:19:22,130 --> 00:19:26,570
So in summary, so transformation
is a first approach,

353
00:19:26,570 --> 00:19:28,810
which seamlessly
unify autoregressive

354
00:19:28,810 --> 00:19:32,130
next token prediction and
the diffusion objective

355
00:19:32,130 --> 00:19:34,730
for multimodal modeling.

356
00:19:34,730 --> 00:19:37,750
It demonstrates a significant
better image generation quality

357
00:19:37,750 --> 00:19:40,230
and token efficiency.

358
00:19:40,230 --> 00:19:43,390
On the other hand, there is
also open research problem.

359
00:19:43,390 --> 00:19:46,030
Because for the
transfusion paper,

360
00:19:46,030 --> 00:19:49,790
it is still trying
to represent--

361
00:19:49,790 --> 00:19:52,950
it is still trying to represent
multimodal information

362
00:19:52,950 --> 00:19:56,110
in text and image
interleaved sequences.

363
00:19:56,110 --> 00:20:03,030
However, it adopts
the VAE representation

364
00:20:03,030 --> 00:20:08,270
for the images, which is
very efficient for generating

365
00:20:08,270 --> 00:20:09,290
the images.

366
00:20:09,290 --> 00:20:10,870
But on the other
hand, these are not

367
00:20:10,870 --> 00:20:14,950
very efficient representation
for image understanding tasks.

368
00:20:14,950 --> 00:20:16,990
So this creates a dilemma.

369
00:20:16,990 --> 00:20:20,750
So which is still
an open problem,

370
00:20:20,750 --> 00:20:22,570
I would say, in the
research field today.

371
00:20:22,570 --> 00:20:25,990
Basically, the field found
that for image generation

372
00:20:25,990 --> 00:20:30,110
and understanding, we sometimes
need two types of encodings

373
00:20:30,110 --> 00:20:31,390
for them.

374
00:20:31,390 --> 00:20:36,220
So state of the art
models today often

375
00:20:36,220 --> 00:20:40,180
tries to overcome this
shortcoming by adopting

376
00:20:40,180 --> 00:20:41,840
two types of image encodings.

377
00:20:41,840 --> 00:20:45,620

378
00:20:45,620 --> 00:20:46,520
Cool.

379
00:20:46,520 --> 00:20:50,160
So we talk about two
types of Omni models,

380
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:52,060
like using different
representation

381
00:20:52,060 --> 00:20:54,740
for the image modality.

382
00:20:54,740 --> 00:20:59,420
So what else can we further
innovate on this architecture

383
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:01,580
to basically to make
them more efficient?

384
00:21:01,580 --> 00:21:05,500
So next, I'm going to talk
about a technique which

385
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:09,420
modifies the transformer
backbone architecture.

386
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:13,380
So basically, the
motivation comes from that.

387
00:21:13,380 --> 00:21:16,540
Now we are having the
transformers process multimodal

388
00:21:16,540 --> 00:21:17,620
information.

389
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:20,740
However different modalities
have different information

390
00:21:20,740 --> 00:21:22,340
density.

391
00:21:22,340 --> 00:21:24,740
The nature of the data
is also very different

392
00:21:24,740 --> 00:21:29,660
when it comes to text
and images, for example.

393
00:21:29,660 --> 00:21:32,140
So the question
is, is it necessary

394
00:21:32,140 --> 00:21:35,520
that we have to use
unified transformer

395
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:40,300
parameters to process
these modalities,

396
00:21:40,300 --> 00:21:43,320
or can we actually introduce
something specialized

397
00:21:43,320 --> 00:21:45,520
to each modality?

398
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:51,920
So this basically takes us to
an architecture which we call

399
00:21:51,920 --> 00:21:54,000
the mixture of transformers.

400
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:57,440
So basically, the idea behind
the mixture of transformers

401
00:21:57,440 --> 00:22:02,240
is we try to come up with
independent set of transformer

402
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:05,000
parameters for each
of the modalities.

403
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:06,960
So by independent
set of parameters,

404
00:22:06,960 --> 00:22:12,320
I'm referring to such as
[INAUDIBLE] projection matrices

405
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:16,600
in the attention layer, as
well as the feed forward layer.

406
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:19,320
So basically, we have
an independent set

407
00:22:19,320 --> 00:22:21,960
of parameters for transformer
parameters for each

408
00:22:21,960 --> 00:22:23,120
of the modality.

409
00:22:23,120 --> 00:22:26,460
For the text modality,
as shown here,

410
00:22:26,460 --> 00:22:30,800
given an input sequence
with interleaved modalities,

411
00:22:30,800 --> 00:22:37,020
we basically select to
activate the set of parameters

412
00:22:37,020 --> 00:22:39,600
based on the modality
deterministically.

413
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:41,940
If it is a text
token, we activate

414
00:22:41,940 --> 00:22:44,880
the text set of parameters.

415
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:48,360
If it is an image token, we
activate the image parameters.

416
00:22:48,360 --> 00:22:54,260
If it's the audio token, we
activate the speech parameters.

417
00:22:54,260 --> 00:23:00,820
So as shown here, we performed
this in the attention layer.

418
00:23:00,820 --> 00:23:04,740
Once we get the QKV
projection separately,

419
00:23:04,740 --> 00:23:07,580
we perform a joint attention
over these modality

420
00:23:07,580 --> 00:23:11,620
tokens to allow them to unify.

421
00:23:11,620 --> 00:23:14,900
Once we get the unified output
from the attention layer,

422
00:23:14,900 --> 00:23:17,320
we pass them through
the feedforward layer.

423
00:23:17,320 --> 00:23:20,920
And the feedforward layer here,
again, is divided by modality.

424
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:25,580
So each modality will have its
own feedforward parameters.

425
00:23:25,580 --> 00:23:28,620
And once we pass these tokens
through the feedforward layer,

426
00:23:28,620 --> 00:23:31,450
we get the normal
transformer output.

427
00:23:31,450 --> 00:23:33,610
So basically from
the outside, we still

428
00:23:33,610 --> 00:23:35,390
have a transformer unit.

429
00:23:35,390 --> 00:23:37,830
We get the token
input and output.

430
00:23:37,830 --> 00:23:42,010
But on the inside, the
processing for each modality

431
00:23:42,010 --> 00:23:45,150
happens differently, using
different set of parameters.

432
00:23:45,150 --> 00:23:48,410

433
00:23:48,410 --> 00:23:51,130
And this technique
can basically can

434
00:23:51,130 --> 00:23:54,170
be combined by with
the two models I just

435
00:23:54,170 --> 00:23:57,890
talked about with the
Chameleon models, which is,

436
00:23:57,890 --> 00:24:03,130
using autoregressive
cross-entropy objective for each

437
00:24:03,130 --> 00:24:04,770
of the modality tokens.

438
00:24:04,770 --> 00:24:07,730
Or with the
transformation technique,

439
00:24:07,730 --> 00:24:11,050
which is using autoregressive
objective for text

440
00:24:11,050 --> 00:24:13,750
and the diffusion
objective for images.

441
00:24:13,750 --> 00:24:16,890

442
00:24:16,890 --> 00:24:20,010
So I'm going to show some
experiments we conducted

443
00:24:20,010 --> 00:24:21,330
with this architecture.

444
00:24:21,330 --> 00:24:26,490
So basically, we did a
scaling ladder for this one.

445
00:24:26,490 --> 00:24:30,070
And we scanned them.

446
00:24:30,070 --> 00:24:33,010
We trained them for
different model sizes.

447
00:24:33,010 --> 00:24:37,510
Like from the smallest, which
is 163 million parameters,

448
00:24:37,510 --> 00:24:40,350
to the largest, which is
seven billion parameters.

449
00:24:40,350 --> 00:24:44,790
So we always compare them with
basically the dense baseline.

450
00:24:44,790 --> 00:24:48,230
And notice that if we
directly compare it

451
00:24:48,230 --> 00:24:51,430
with a dense baseline,
basically mixture transformers

452
00:24:51,430 --> 00:24:56,110
will have an advantage, because
it has double the parameters.

453
00:24:56,110 --> 00:25:00,830
So we also compared with a
baseline which is basically

454
00:25:00,830 --> 00:25:04,110
a mixture of expert model.

455
00:25:04,110 --> 00:25:07,110
So which has roughly
the same amount

456
00:25:07,110 --> 00:25:10,610
of total parameters
as the MoT model.

457
00:25:10,610 --> 00:25:13,550

458
00:25:13,550 --> 00:25:15,810
So here are some results.

459
00:25:15,810 --> 00:25:19,990
So basically, we show
the training loss.

460
00:25:19,990 --> 00:25:24,850
And also the validation on
the image modality generation.

461
00:25:24,850 --> 00:25:26,630
So we show the validation loss.

462
00:25:26,630 --> 00:25:30,330
We also show some
basically sampling

463
00:25:30,330 --> 00:25:34,090
based image generation
evaluation metrics.

464
00:25:34,090 --> 00:25:38,330
So one thing we found is that
the mixture of the transformer

465
00:25:38,330 --> 00:25:41,730
technique is especially
effective at improve

466
00:25:41,730 --> 00:25:44,190
the generation of
non-text modalities.

467
00:25:44,190 --> 00:25:47,830
So here, I didn't show the
text modality generation.

468
00:25:47,830 --> 00:25:49,110
You can refer to the paper.

469
00:25:49,110 --> 00:25:52,690
But in the paper, we found
that actually MoT architecture

470
00:25:52,690 --> 00:25:56,130
will demonstrate similar
comparable text performance

471
00:25:56,130 --> 00:25:57,730
as a dense variation.

472
00:25:57,730 --> 00:26:02,410
So what it really shines it is
able to, without sacrificing

473
00:26:02,410 --> 00:26:05,590
the text performance, achieving
much better performance,

474
00:26:05,590 --> 00:26:08,970
generating non-text
modalities such as images,

475
00:26:08,970 --> 00:26:09,910
as is shown here.

476
00:26:09,910 --> 00:26:12,010
So here, you can
see the right curve,

477
00:26:12,010 --> 00:26:16,570
basically MoT will have a much
lower image generation loss.

478
00:26:16,570 --> 00:26:18,890
And also on the
sample evaluation,

479
00:26:18,890 --> 00:26:21,650
it will show much better
performance compared

480
00:26:21,650 --> 00:26:23,450
to the dense variation.

481
00:26:23,450 --> 00:26:31,720
So an explanation for this is
non-text modality generation

482
00:26:31,720 --> 00:26:36,240
is probably something unique
compared to text generation.

483
00:26:36,240 --> 00:26:39,760
So if we put everything
inside a single transformer,

484
00:26:39,760 --> 00:26:43,600
there might be severe
capacity computation.

485
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:47,560
But if we can allow
these separate abilities

486
00:26:47,560 --> 00:26:49,720
with different
set of parameters,

487
00:26:49,720 --> 00:26:51,700
we are able to scale
them much better.

488
00:26:51,700 --> 00:26:56,560

489
00:26:56,560 --> 00:26:59,480
So here are some
qualitative results.

490
00:26:59,480 --> 00:27:05,240
So basically, we show that
so for the top line, which

491
00:27:05,240 --> 00:27:07,880
is the dense baseline,
and the bottom line

492
00:27:07,880 --> 00:27:10,080
is like the mixture of
transformer architecture

493
00:27:10,080 --> 00:27:11,340
for image generation.

494
00:27:11,340 --> 00:27:15,200
So basically, we found
that across many examples,

495
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:17,480
the MoT architecture
is basically able

496
00:27:17,480 --> 00:27:21,600
to follow fine-grained
instructions much better

497
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:24,540
and generate much
detailed objects.

498
00:27:24,540 --> 00:27:28,020

499
00:27:28,020 --> 00:27:31,540
So here is just to mention
that the mixture of transformer

500
00:27:31,540 --> 00:27:35,240
technique can also be combined
with mixture of experts.

501
00:27:35,240 --> 00:27:37,980

502
00:27:37,980 --> 00:27:42,160
So before I was saying
for each of the modality,

503
00:27:42,160 --> 00:27:45,620
we keep one copy of the
transformer parameters.

504
00:27:45,620 --> 00:27:48,660
But we can also use the
mixture of expert technique

505
00:27:48,660 --> 00:27:49,480
to scale them.

506
00:27:49,480 --> 00:27:51,700
So for example, we
can choose to allocate

507
00:27:51,700 --> 00:27:55,940
different amount of experts
to different modalities.

508
00:27:55,940 --> 00:27:59,780
So I think we used to find
that allocating more experts

509
00:27:59,780 --> 00:28:03,100
is especially helpful
for tax performance.

510
00:28:03,100 --> 00:28:07,500
While for abilities such
as image generation,

511
00:28:07,500 --> 00:28:11,620
usually it doesn't
scale as fast as text

512
00:28:11,620 --> 00:28:13,640
when you're adding
the number of experts.

513
00:28:13,640 --> 00:28:14,720
So which motivates us.

514
00:28:14,720 --> 00:28:18,660
We can potentially have
customized number of experts

515
00:28:18,660 --> 00:28:20,000
for different modalities.

516
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:23,940

517
00:28:23,940 --> 00:28:24,710
Cool.

518
00:28:24,710 --> 00:28:28,690
So to summarize, so
basically the mixture

519
00:28:28,690 --> 00:28:30,970
of transformer technique
I talked about,

520
00:28:30,970 --> 00:28:35,770
it is a technique which employs
modality specific parameters.

521
00:28:35,770 --> 00:28:39,210
And it performs deterministic
routing on the tokens

522
00:28:39,210 --> 00:28:42,250
when passing them
through the transformer.

523
00:28:42,250 --> 00:28:46,450
These techniques, we found it to
be able to substantively improve

524
00:28:46,450 --> 00:28:49,330
generation quality for
non-text modalities,

525
00:28:49,330 --> 00:28:51,930
including image and speech.

526
00:28:51,930 --> 00:28:55,210
And we found that it's
also a technique which

527
00:28:55,210 --> 00:28:59,570
is helpful to enable the overall
stability and controllability

528
00:28:59,570 --> 00:29:01,970
of mixed model
training, because now we

529
00:29:01,970 --> 00:29:05,150
have different set of parameters
for different modalities.

530
00:29:05,150 --> 00:29:09,370
This can actually allow us to
perform asynchronous training

531
00:29:09,370 --> 00:29:11,250
for these modalities.

532
00:29:11,250 --> 00:29:13,810
So a common scenario
people use it

533
00:29:13,810 --> 00:29:17,490
is suppose we have some
off the shelf text models

534
00:29:17,490 --> 00:29:21,750
already been built. It has
very strong text capabilities.

535
00:29:21,750 --> 00:29:24,350
And now we want to add, for
example, image generation

536
00:29:24,350 --> 00:29:26,230
or speech generation
to this model.

537
00:29:26,230 --> 00:29:29,790
So then instead of
performing full fine tuning

538
00:29:29,790 --> 00:29:34,430
or continuous training on this
existing off the shelf language

539
00:29:34,430 --> 00:29:38,630
model, what's possible is to add
an additional set of parameters

540
00:29:38,630 --> 00:29:40,790
for image or speech to it.

541
00:29:40,790 --> 00:29:43,570
And then we can
freeze the text model,

542
00:29:43,570 --> 00:29:45,830
keeping its text
capability unchanged

543
00:29:45,830 --> 00:29:49,750
and just training the image
and speech parameters.

544
00:29:49,750 --> 00:29:52,190
So this basically allows
a simple technique

545
00:29:52,190 --> 00:29:56,910
to extend existing models with
additional modality generation.

546
00:29:56,910 --> 00:29:57,896
Question?

547
00:29:57,896 --> 00:30:01,347
[INAUDIBLE]

548
00:30:01,347 --> 00:30:02,830

549
00:30:02,830 --> 00:30:06,550
--the generation of images
are two different systems.

550
00:30:06,550 --> 00:30:10,710
For example, the text to image
is more like large language

551
00:30:10,710 --> 00:30:15,890
models and image to text is--
no, it's another way around.

552
00:30:15,890 --> 00:30:18,550
It's like image to text is more
like large language models,

553
00:30:18,550 --> 00:30:22,250
and text to image is more
like diffusion models.

554
00:30:22,250 --> 00:30:26,290
But it's multimodality, meaning
that you want to unify these,

555
00:30:26,290 --> 00:30:31,690
having a single model to achieve
the best in both directions.

556
00:30:31,690 --> 00:30:34,750
Is there a loop, image to
text and image and things?

557
00:30:34,750 --> 00:30:35,297
Yes.

558
00:30:35,297 --> 00:30:36,630
So this is a very good question.

559
00:30:36,630 --> 00:30:40,830
So basically, the
question asked about--

560
00:30:40,830 --> 00:30:44,730
so image understanding,
which is more image to text,

561
00:30:44,730 --> 00:30:47,310
versus image generation,
which is more text to images.

562
00:30:47,310 --> 00:30:49,610
They seem to be
different capabilities.

563
00:30:49,610 --> 00:30:52,370
We hear talking about having
a single transformer which

564
00:30:52,370 --> 00:30:54,930
is doing well in terms of both.

565
00:30:54,930 --> 00:30:56,290
So this is a great question.

566
00:30:56,290 --> 00:30:59,230
Actually, it is not.

567
00:30:59,230 --> 00:31:01,650
So here, when I'm
talking, so in the mixture

568
00:31:01,650 --> 00:31:05,690
of transformer context, when
I talk about image modality,

569
00:31:05,690 --> 00:31:08,290
I'm referring to
image generation.

570
00:31:08,290 --> 00:31:15,130
So I think we actually didn't
found modality separation

571
00:31:15,130 --> 00:31:18,410
to help image understanding.

572
00:31:18,410 --> 00:31:20,760
So it seems that
so the capability

573
00:31:20,760 --> 00:31:23,560
is bounded by whether you are
outputting text or outputting

574
00:31:23,560 --> 00:31:24,260
images.

575
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:28,560
But if you're outputting
text, your input tokens better

576
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:31,100
go through the same
set of parameters,

577
00:31:31,100 --> 00:31:34,440
whether they are text or images.

578
00:31:34,440 --> 00:31:36,380
Yeah, so that's
a great question.

579
00:31:36,380 --> 00:31:39,000

580
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:42,120
Yep, so and finally,
just to mention,

581
00:31:42,120 --> 00:31:44,720
since this publication,
so MoT has inspired

582
00:31:44,720 --> 00:31:49,600
a growing line of follow-up work
in modality aware architectures.

583
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:54,560
So I'm going to quickly
talk a few work done

584
00:31:54,560 --> 00:32:00,080
by other people, which inherited
the MoT modeling architecture.

585
00:32:00,080 --> 00:32:01,800
So the first one I'm
going to talk about

586
00:32:01,800 --> 00:32:04,840
is actually related to
the question just now.

587
00:32:04,840 --> 00:32:10,880
So basically in last, there
was a very strong Omni model

588
00:32:10,880 --> 00:32:12,560
came out named BAGEL.

589
00:32:12,560 --> 00:32:18,040
So BAGEL employed architecture
very closely related to MoT

590
00:32:18,040 --> 00:32:20,820
So basically, they have
separate set of parameters

591
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:22,580
for image generation.

592
00:32:22,580 --> 00:32:24,780
But on the other
hand, their base model

593
00:32:24,780 --> 00:32:28,220
is actually a multimodal
language model.

594
00:32:28,220 --> 00:32:30,980
So basically, they have a
separate set of parameters

595
00:32:30,980 --> 00:32:32,720
for image generation.

596
00:32:32,720 --> 00:32:35,740
But for the understanding
part, the understanding part

597
00:32:35,740 --> 00:32:40,180
can actually take in
both text and images.

598
00:32:40,180 --> 00:32:42,420
So basically, BAGEL was
using a different set

599
00:32:42,420 --> 00:32:46,780
of encoding representations for
image understanding features.

600
00:32:46,780 --> 00:32:49,860
And also image understanding
and image generation

601
00:32:49,860 --> 00:32:51,940
goes through a separate
set of parameters.

602
00:32:51,940 --> 00:32:56,300
So I think this is also related
to a problem I just mentioned

603
00:32:56,300 --> 00:32:58,540
before, which is the
field haven't yet

604
00:32:58,540 --> 00:33:00,520
found a very effective way.

605
00:33:00,520 --> 00:33:03,260
So there's currently
ongoing research.

606
00:33:03,260 --> 00:33:05,660
But I think right
now, we don't have

607
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:08,820
a very effective way to
unify image generation

608
00:33:08,820 --> 00:33:11,260
and understanding.

609
00:33:11,260 --> 00:33:14,580
So here, one
interesting capability

610
00:33:14,580 --> 00:33:18,320
shown in the BAGEL
paper is because recall,

611
00:33:18,320 --> 00:33:22,440
we are modeling
mixed model sequences

612
00:33:22,440 --> 00:33:25,780
as an autoregressive generation.

613
00:33:25,780 --> 00:33:27,800
So it unlocks
capabilities such as we

614
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:31,105
can allow the model to plan
before it generates an image.

615
00:33:31,105 --> 00:33:32,480
So basically, the
model can first

616
00:33:32,480 --> 00:33:36,040
generate thinking tracing
text and then generate

617
00:33:36,040 --> 00:33:37,140
the final image.

618
00:33:37,140 --> 00:33:39,960
So the paper show
that this would

619
00:33:39,960 --> 00:33:44,040
allow the models to generate
images with much better details.

620
00:33:44,040 --> 00:33:48,120
And I think it's also
basically thinking

621
00:33:48,120 --> 00:33:50,280
before generation is
also the technique, which

622
00:33:50,280 --> 00:33:53,840
is used by lots of the state of
the art image generation models

623
00:33:53,840 --> 00:33:54,540
nowadays.

624
00:33:54,540 --> 00:33:57,080

625
00:33:57,080 --> 00:34:01,480
So another type of adoption
for the mixture of transformer

626
00:34:01,480 --> 00:34:06,040
architecture was actually in
the embodied AI community.

627
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:08,880
So basically, it's very
interesting that we found--

628
00:34:08,880 --> 00:34:11,380
so it wasn't included
in our original paper.

629
00:34:11,380 --> 00:34:16,560
But we actually found
that in robotics actually,

630
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:22,550
lots of current architectures
will use MoT style architecture

631
00:34:22,550 --> 00:34:25,510
to predict the action vectors.

632
00:34:25,510 --> 00:34:27,690
So basically instead
of generating images,

633
00:34:27,690 --> 00:34:32,070
so for these type of problems,
action is a different modality.

634
00:34:32,070 --> 00:34:34,110
And basically, these
models will adopt

635
00:34:34,110 --> 00:34:37,389
like an extra special set of
parameters for action prediction

636
00:34:37,389 --> 00:34:40,150
using an architecture
very similar to MoT.

637
00:34:40,150 --> 00:34:43,949
So again, basically,
the idea is while it

638
00:34:43,949 --> 00:34:46,230
is having a separate
set of architecture,

639
00:34:46,230 --> 00:34:49,070
the overall language
modeling structure

640
00:34:49,070 --> 00:34:51,150
and the self-attention
in the transformer

641
00:34:51,150 --> 00:34:55,030
will allow the base language
models, world knowledge,

642
00:34:55,030 --> 00:34:59,950
and so on to transform
to the action prediction,

643
00:34:59,950 --> 00:35:02,490
which will make the
prediction much better.

644
00:35:02,490 --> 00:35:05,550

645
00:35:05,550 --> 00:35:07,170
Cool.

646
00:35:07,170 --> 00:35:07,930
So yes.

647
00:35:07,930 --> 00:35:11,230
So here, I actually
also have a special set

648
00:35:11,230 --> 00:35:14,310
of slides dedicated to the
question we just discussed.

649
00:35:14,310 --> 00:35:16,730
So basically their
positive transfer

650
00:35:16,730 --> 00:35:19,970
between understanding and
generation of the non-text

651
00:35:19,970 --> 00:35:21,650
modalities.

652
00:35:21,650 --> 00:35:27,350
So here, I think we need to
see them in two directions.

653
00:35:27,350 --> 00:35:30,490
So basically, in terms
of better understanding

654
00:35:30,490 --> 00:35:33,870
can help generation, the
answer is strongly, yes.

655
00:35:33,870 --> 00:35:36,330
So basically, people
found that if we

656
00:35:36,330 --> 00:35:41,270
build a better understanding
capabilities in the base model,

657
00:35:41,270 --> 00:35:45,090
so this will allow the model
to have much better information

658
00:35:45,090 --> 00:35:48,630
processing ability, planning
ability, reasoning ability.

659
00:35:48,630 --> 00:35:52,770
So it will allow us to
generate images, which are

660
00:35:52,770 --> 00:35:54,510
much more fine grained details.

661
00:35:54,510 --> 00:35:57,570
And for infographics, it
can generate infographics

662
00:35:57,570 --> 00:36:01,410
with more accurate information,
less hallucination.

663
00:36:01,410 --> 00:36:05,530
But on the other hand,
suppose we take Omni model

664
00:36:05,530 --> 00:36:09,090
and we further train them
for generation capabilities

665
00:36:09,090 --> 00:36:12,250
on the non-text modalities.

666
00:36:12,250 --> 00:36:13,970
There has been
little work showing

667
00:36:13,970 --> 00:36:16,750
that this has a positive
transfer for understanding

668
00:36:16,750 --> 00:36:17,450
capability.

669
00:36:17,450 --> 00:36:22,350
So in plain words, which is if
we train the model for image

670
00:36:22,350 --> 00:36:26,210
generation and we train it
on a lot of token budget,

671
00:36:26,210 --> 00:36:28,950
it doesn't necessarily
improve the model's ability

672
00:36:28,950 --> 00:36:32,390
to perform image
understanding tasks.

673
00:36:32,390 --> 00:36:35,110
Which is very interesting.

674
00:36:35,110 --> 00:36:38,070
So I think related is last year.

675
00:36:38,070 --> 00:36:41,790
So basically, the Berkeley
professor Sergey Levine

676
00:36:41,790 --> 00:36:44,570
also tweeted on his account.

677
00:36:44,570 --> 00:36:48,950
Basically, he mentioned that
it is puzzling that we can--

678
00:36:48,950 --> 00:36:53,390
basically, language model can
acquire such amazing abilities

679
00:36:53,390 --> 00:36:55,430
through next token prediction.

680
00:36:55,430 --> 00:36:59,870
But on the other hand, we don't
make video models much stronger

681
00:36:59,870 --> 00:37:02,430
if we do next frame prediction.

682
00:37:02,430 --> 00:37:06,630
So this is a very interesting
phenomena people have observed

683
00:37:06,630 --> 00:37:09,450
and a couple of explanations.

684
00:37:09,450 --> 00:37:13,260
They are a little bit
hand-wavy, which is also like--

685
00:37:13,260 --> 00:37:16,820
some of them is also included
in Sergey's original post.

686
00:37:16,820 --> 00:37:21,460
So one hypothesis is
language is fundamentally

687
00:37:21,460 --> 00:37:23,660
different from the
other modalities,

688
00:37:23,660 --> 00:37:26,140
because it is a highly
compressed abstraction

689
00:37:26,140 --> 00:37:27,740
of human cognition.

690
00:37:27,740 --> 00:37:30,640
So when we are training on
language next token prediction,

691
00:37:30,640 --> 00:37:34,460
we are actually already trained
on basically human's reasoning

692
00:37:34,460 --> 00:37:35,120
process.

693
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:38,380
And also some of the
words we see also

694
00:37:38,380 --> 00:37:41,580
implies actions we
are going to take.

695
00:37:41,580 --> 00:37:44,880
Versus in comparison,
the images and videos,

696
00:37:44,880 --> 00:37:46,900
they are sensory data.

697
00:37:46,900 --> 00:37:50,720
They are not our subjective
interpretation of the word.

698
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:52,980
But instead, they are
the passive observation

699
00:37:52,980 --> 00:37:53,900
of the world.

700
00:37:53,900 --> 00:37:58,220
So training on them, we will
have very different consequences

701
00:37:58,220 --> 00:38:00,540
compared if we
train on languages.

702
00:38:00,540 --> 00:38:04,900
And also for image and
videos, the loss landscape

703
00:38:04,900 --> 00:38:08,780
is more complicated.

704
00:38:08,780 --> 00:38:11,180
So there has been some
experiments basically

705
00:38:11,180 --> 00:38:16,720
showing that even when the image
and video generation is not

706
00:38:16,720 --> 00:38:20,120
looking great to humans,
their loss might have already

707
00:38:20,120 --> 00:38:21,620
been starting to look good.

708
00:38:21,620 --> 00:38:25,600
So basically, designing the loss
landscape for images and videos

709
00:38:25,600 --> 00:38:27,760
are also more complicated.

710
00:38:27,760 --> 00:38:30,300
And there's also
other hypotheses.

711
00:38:30,300 --> 00:38:32,560
For example, if we just
look at the real world,

712
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:34,840
next frame prediction
would be very redundant.

713
00:38:34,840 --> 00:38:38,360
Because there will be lots
of redundant information

714
00:38:38,360 --> 00:38:41,720
and replication
across video frames,

715
00:38:41,720 --> 00:38:44,340
which could possibly
contribute to this.

716
00:38:44,340 --> 00:38:48,880
But I think overall, this is
a very interesting phenomenon

717
00:38:48,880 --> 00:38:51,880
worth bearing in
mind, which could also

718
00:38:51,880 --> 00:38:56,000
point to us that although
we see lots of success,

719
00:38:56,000 --> 00:38:58,520
applying learnings from
large language modeling

720
00:38:58,520 --> 00:39:03,720
to multimodal modeling,
this is likely not the end.

721
00:39:03,720 --> 00:39:06,280
So there is much more
fundamental problems

722
00:39:06,280 --> 00:39:11,270
to solve when it comes
to multimodal models.

723
00:39:11,270 --> 00:39:12,270
Yeah.

724
00:39:12,270 --> 00:39:14,750
So here is basically
a summary slides.

725
00:39:14,750 --> 00:39:19,430
So basically what it
says is the models

726
00:39:19,430 --> 00:39:22,630
I just presented, which
I call Omni language

727
00:39:22,630 --> 00:39:24,990
models, like
Chameleon, transfusion,

728
00:39:24,990 --> 00:39:28,310
mixture of experts,
so they solve

729
00:39:28,310 --> 00:39:31,510
a small subset of the
multimodal intelligence

730
00:39:31,510 --> 00:39:33,210
problems we face today.

731
00:39:33,210 --> 00:39:37,270
So they especially excel
at multimodal information

732
00:39:37,270 --> 00:39:39,030
processing.

733
00:39:39,030 --> 00:39:43,010
But when it comes to
the wild physical world,

734
00:39:43,010 --> 00:39:46,530
when we talk about things like
spatial temporal understanding,

735
00:39:46,530 --> 00:39:49,630
real time understanding,
robotics control,

736
00:39:49,630 --> 00:39:53,010
there are still lots of
unaddressed problems,

737
00:39:53,010 --> 00:39:56,590
and people are still dedicated
to understanding and coming up

738
00:39:56,590 --> 00:40:00,970
with better representations for
these multi-modal information

739
00:40:00,970 --> 00:40:03,710
when you come to real
world processing.

740
00:40:03,710 --> 00:40:06,630
With this, I also want
to conclude my talk.

741
00:40:06,630 --> 00:40:10,770
So basically, I hope
this talk has motivated

742
00:40:10,770 --> 00:40:14,370
that building models with
native multimodal capabilities

743
00:40:14,370 --> 00:40:16,950
is a very active
research field nowadays.

744
00:40:16,950 --> 00:40:18,830
It has a ton of open problems.

745
00:40:18,830 --> 00:40:22,810
It's not as figured
out as language models.

746
00:40:22,810 --> 00:40:26,610
And for today, lots
of multimodal language

747
00:40:26,610 --> 00:40:31,650
models we see, they excel at
digital information processing.

748
00:40:31,650 --> 00:40:35,250
But we still are far
away from a paradigm

749
00:40:35,250 --> 00:40:39,010
which could perform powerful
physical world multimodal

750
00:40:39,010 --> 00:40:41,490
intelligence.

751
00:40:41,490 --> 00:40:44,830
Multimodal models
compare with text models,

752
00:40:44,830 --> 00:40:47,210
they are more
computationally heavy,

753
00:40:47,210 --> 00:40:50,690
which requires additional
challenges to be solved in terms

754
00:40:50,690 --> 00:40:55,050
of training and infrastructure.

755
00:40:55,050 --> 00:41:00,330
And also, multimodal itself is a
very complicated and interesting

756
00:41:00,330 --> 00:41:02,610
problem space.

757
00:41:02,610 --> 00:41:08,070
So like we just mentioned,
digital information

758
00:41:08,070 --> 00:41:11,150
understanding would require
multimodal processing.

759
00:41:11,150 --> 00:41:13,890
Physical intelligence would
require multimodal processing.

760
00:41:13,890 --> 00:41:16,330
Robotics would require
multimodal processing.

761
00:41:16,330 --> 00:41:19,230
So in the short
term, we will likely

762
00:41:19,230 --> 00:41:22,630
see an increasing number of
multimodal models customized

763
